WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:13.063
Most people are familiar with the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World―astounding works of art, architecture, and design that have inspired awe and admiration for thousands of years.

2
00:00:13.064 --> 00:00:19.228
They include the Colossus of Rhodes, the Hanging Gardens of Babylon, and the Lighthouse at Alexandria.

3
00:00:19.229 --> 00:00:23.783
Today only one of the ancient wonders remains standing: the Great Pyramids of Giza.

4
00:00:23.784 --> 00:00:32.053
In 1994 the American Society of Civil Engineers wanted to pay tribute to the greatest engineering accomplishments of the modern world.

5
00:00:32.054 --> 00:00:38.498
After consulting engineers the world over, the society decided on a list of Seven Wonders of the Modern World.

6
00:00:38.499 --> 00:00:46.259
This new list was chosen to demonstrate modern society's ability to achieve unachievable feats and reach unreachable heights.

7
00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:57.031
It includes the Empire State Building, the Golden Gate Bridge, the Channel Tunnel, the CN Tower, the Panama Canal, the Itaipu Dam, and the Delta Works in the North Sea.

8
00:00:57.032 --> 00:01:02.483
The Empire State Building is a 102-story skyscraper in downtown New York.

9
00:01:02.484 --> 00:01:13.343
When it was completed in 1931, the Empire State Building became the benchmark for all other skyscrapers that followed because it combined beautiful architecture with solid engineering.

10
00:01:13.344 --> 00:01:19.955
For over fifty years, it was the tallest building in the world, and it remains the tallest building in New York.

11
00:01:19.956 --> 00:01:25.959
In its early days, when many of its offices were vacant, its nickname was the Empty State Building.

12
00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:32.436
Today it is New York's most visited tourist attraction and an unmistakable part of the city's skyline.

13
00:01:32.437 --> 00:01:37.722
In Toronto, Canada, the CN Tower soars over half a kilometer into the sky.

14
00:01:37.723 --> 00:01:42.833
It is twice as high as the Eiffel Tower and is the tallest free-standing structure on land.

15
00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:53.707
It was built in the 1970s when Toronto's radio and TV stations were having difficulty transmitting their broadcasts, due to the fact that many new skyscrapers were blocking the signals.

16
00:01:53.708 --> 00:02:01.854
The transmitters at the peak of the CN Tower now provide residents of Toronto with some of the best radio and television reception in North America.

17
00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:11.674
It may originally have been built for practical reasons, but since it opened in 1976, the CN Tower has become one of Canada's most popular tourist attractions.

18
00:02:11.675 --> 00:02:17.372
Visitors ascend the tower in a glass elevator to an observation deck and a revolving restaurant.

19
00:02:17.373 --> 00:02:23.757
When the weather is clear, they can see across Lake Ontario into New York State. It's quite a view!

20
00:02:23.758 --> 00:02:29.233
Plans for a tunnel linking England and France were first proposed almost two hundred years ago.

21
00:02:29.234 --> 00:02:34.547
But it was not until 1994 that engineers succeeded in building the Channel Tunnel.

22
00:02:34.548 --> 00:02:40.619
For centuries travel between France and England, which are only 34 kilometers apart, was arduous.

23
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.932
The fastest crossing took seven hours; in bad weather it could take weeks.

24
00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:49.717
Today, thanks to the Channel Tunnel, the journey takes only twenty minutes by train.

25
00:02:49.718 --> 00:02:52.695
The construction of the tunnel, however, was not easy.

26
00:02:52.696 --> 00:02:59.187
It took over fifteen thousand workers seven years to build, tunneling from both sides and meeting in the middle.

27
00:02:59.188 --> 00:03:05.499
Not since the Ice Age, which was thirteen thousand years ago, could people walk between the two countries.

28
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.435
The Channel Tunnel is truly a modern wonder.

29
00:03:08.436 --> 00:03:14.769
The Golden Gate Bridge in San Francisco, which opened in 1937, also links two pieces of land.

30
00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:19.623
It spans 1.28 kilometers, making it one of the longest bridges in the world.

31
00:03:19.624 --> 00:03:27.506
Because San Francisco sits next to the Pacific Ocean and the mouth of a harbor, the city frequently experiences dense fog.

32
00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:33.130
So architects chose to paint the bridge a bright orange color for greater visibility.

33
00:03:33.131 --> 00:03:39.804
This orange bridge has become a symbol for the San Francisco area, as well as a famous icon of advanced engineering.

34
00:03:39.805 --> 00:03:45.501
Over the years the Golden Gate Bridge has survived strong winds, ocean currents, and even earthquakes.

35
00:03:45.502 --> 00:03:55.112
Experts never thought that it would last so long in such a turbulent environment, but the bridge has only been closed three times in its entire history due to inclement weather.

36
00:03:55.113 --> 00:04:00.555
Even the strong earthquake that hit the area in 1989 did not damage the Golden Gate Bridge.

37
00:04:00.556 --> 00:04:09.088
Another wonder that crosses water, the Itaipu Dam, is located deep in the forests of South America on the border between Brazil and Paraguay.

38
00:04:09.089 --> 00:04:14.547
It was built in 1982 to supply water to drought-stricken areas of both countries.

39
00:04:14.548 --> 00:04:23.408
Its construction involved redirecting the seventh largest river in the world, the Parana River, and moving over fifty million tons of dirt and rock.

40
00:04:23.409 --> 00:04:26.749
This massive dam is nearly eight kilometers long.

41
00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:31.568
All in all, it required fifteen times more concrete than the Channel Tunnel.

42
00:04:31.569 --> 00:04:41.305
It was selected as a Wonder of the Modern World not just because of the effort it took to build, but also because it houses the largest hydroelectric power plant in the world.

43
00:04:41.306 --> 00:04:47.359
The Delta Works in the Netherlands, the least-known Wonder of the Modern World, are an amazing feat of engineering.

44
00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:54.739
In 1953 high sea levels in the North Sea flooded much of the low-lying country, crashing through dikes and seawalls.

45
00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:59.108
Almost two thousand people were killed and seventy thousand were evacuated.

46
00:04:59.109 --> 00:05:06.832
Engineers immediately began plans to dam four major rivers and tidal inlets to prevent a similar event from happening again.

47
00:05:06.833 --> 00:05:12.267
Twice a day water flows in and out through these inlets, where the rivers widen to meet the sea.

48
00:05:12.268 --> 00:05:24.049
In order to prevent further flooding, engineers had to build a system of locks, channels, and gates to control the flow of this water so that it would always be directed out to sea, not onto neighboring land.

49
00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:29.912
This was a difficult process that began in 1958 and took more than thirty years to complete.

50
00:05:29.913 --> 00:05:35.536
Today the Delta Works ensures that the people of the Netherlands are protected from rising sea levels.

51
00:05:35.537 --> 00:05:39.811
The Panama Canal has the longest history of all the Wonders.

52
00:05:39.812 --> 00:05:51.904
Nearly five hundred years ago in 1534, Charles I of Spain proposed the construction of a canal that would link the Atlantic Ocean north of Colon, Panama, to the Pacific Ocean, south of Panama City.

53
00:05:51.905 --> 00:05:59.292
Work on the canal didn't actually start until centuries later; it began in 1881 and was completed in 1914.

54
00:05:59.293 --> 00:06:07.547
The Panama Canal has a series of locks to compensate for a difference of 5.9 meters between the levels of the Pacific and Atlantic oceans.

55
00:06:07.548 --> 00:06:13.601
When a ship travels through from the Pacific Ocean, it enters the first of many sequential locks.

56
00:06:13.602 --> 00:06:17.829
Each one is flooded to gradually raise the water level higher and higher.

57
00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:24.993
When the lock water level reaches the same height as the water on the other side, the ship is released and it may sail out into the next lock.

58
00:06:24.994 --> 00:06:30.701
It takes ships about nine hours to journey through all of the locks, finally reaching the Atlantic Ocean.

59
00:06:30.702 --> 00:06:34.487
Approximately twelve thousand ships travel through it each year.

60
00:06:34.488 --> 00:06:44.536
As the members of the American Society of Civil Engineers said, "The original wonders were amazing sites to behold, but today's modern wonders are more than simply awe inspiring.

61
00:06:44.537 --> 00:06:50.425
They are operational masterpieces that have revolutionized civil engineering and benefited humanity."

62
00:06:50.426 --> 00:06:57.153
But surely there are other superb examples of engineering and design that have not been included in this list.

63
00:06:57.154 --> 00:07:02.676
Don't worryᅳanother list called the Forgotten Modern Wonders of the World has been compiled.

64
00:07:02.677 --> 00:07:13.728
It includes famous landmarks, such as the Eiffel Tower in Paris; the clock in London known as Big Ben; the Petronas Towers in Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia; and the Aswan High Dam in Egypt.

65
00:07:13.729 --> 00:07:20.553
The next time you take a trip, try to visit one of the Wonders of the Modern World and be inspired by human ingenuity.
